abstract: "Chan, Crommelin and Mandjes conjectured that every vanishing compartment of their interaction-driven spreading models decays exponentially when two graphs built from the model are acyclic or nearly so. An earlier Evidence Press release (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23501636) refuted the conjecture with a four-compartment 18/t^2 example. This successor gives a four-compartment model in which both graphs are acyclic and the trajectory is explicit, with x(t) ~ sqrt(3/8) t^(-3/2); a no-direct-infection SWIR-type submodel also gives a t^(-3/2) counterexample. With four compartments nothing decays more slowly than t^(-3/2); chains give t^(-1-1/m) for every even m. Algebraic extinction needs non-generic initial data in single-catalyst models, exponential decay returns after perturbation by an explicit slowing law, the slow tails never follow an outbreak peak in the chain, SWIR and hierarchy families, and support- and rate-checkable conditions rule the mechanism out. Unrefereed candidate. Original code is MIT-licensed; see LICENSES.md."
